  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consistent with the Article III of the SBAA [or the Supplemental Provisions to the Project Document], the responsibility for the safety and security of the Implementing Partner and its personnel and property, and of UNDP&#x2019;s property in the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s custody, rests with the Implementing Partner.  To this end, the Implementing Partner shall: a)	put in place an appropriate security plan and maintain the security plan, taking into account the security situation in the country where the project is being carried; b)	assume all risks and liabilities related to the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s security, and the full implementation of the security plan.</narrative>
   </condition>
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and environmental sustainability will be enhanced through application of the UNDP Social and Environmental Standards (http://www.undp.org/ses) and related Accountability Mechanism (http://www.undp.org/secu-srm).</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1 % of committees that conduct effective bill scrutiny in line with relevant guidelines
1.2 Number of consultations between committees and CSOs, academia and research institutions participating in and engaging with committees in terms of the relevant CSO guidelines.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2017">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) 12% of committees that conducts effective bill scrutiny
2) Capacity of Parliament to network with key stakeholders during law making process was 13.4% 
3) The capacity for budget committee to scrutinize bills was at 16%  
4) Low quality and fewer field visits depending on availability of funds</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- 12 sectoral committees have been oriented with relevant scrutiny guidelines and use the knowledge to conduct an evidence-based detailed review of Bills 

- At least 7 consultations held between selected committees and relevant CSOs, Academia, Research Institutions, and citizens</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- 12 sectoral committees conduct evidence-based detailed review of 21 Bills each with recommendations for improvement

- 62 e-consultations held between selected committees and relevant CSOs, Academia, Research institutions and citizens</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- 10 sectoral committees conduct an evidence-based detailed review of at least 3 Bills each with recommendations for improvement
-At least 6 consultations held between selected committees and relevant CSOs, Academia, Research Institutions and citizens</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- The project supported six committees to scrutinize 12 Bills ((The Plant Health Bill, 2020; and Deep Sea Fisheries Management &amp; Development Bill 2020, The e-Government Bill 2019, The Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) (No. 4) Bill, 2019, The Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) (No. 5) Bill 2019, The Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) (No. 6) Bill, 2019, The Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) (No.7) Bill, 2019, The Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) (No.8) Bill, 2019, The Arbitration Bill, 2020, The Appropriation Bill, 2020, The Finance Bill, 2020, Budget Proposal 2020/2021 and the Written Laws (Miscellaneous Amendments) Bill, 2020 ) which were presented before the Parliament.
- In response to the Covid-19 pandemic, the National Assembly took some precaution measures which included restriction to physical contacts (social distancing). Alternatively, virtual public hearings were utilized which enabled only 3 consultations to be held instead of the planned 6, however, that attracted over 257 CSOs to submit written opinions on the Bills under consideration. This is equal to 42% increase as compared to the year 2019 which had 27% only.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
